The script filters out both "../" and "natas_webpass" making direct access
to the natas26 password impossible.  However, it does log requests and
stores the User-Agent field from requests directly into the log file.  We
can maliciously set this to PHP code that dumps out the file we want.
Then, since the log file itself is /tmp/natas25_<session_id>.log, we
can access the log file through the main PHP script by setting the lang
field to the file in /tmp.  Note that we still must bypass the "../"
filtering in str_replace.  To do so, we must understand that str_replace
does a single pass from left to right which allows us to inject chars
in a way that ../ is generated by str_replace.  Loading up the log file
then gives us the following

----
[29.11.2014 17::22:54] oGgWAJ7zcGT28vYazGo4rkhOPDhBu34T
 "Directory traversal attempt! fixing request."
<br />
---

password for natas26 is oGgWAJ7zcGT28vYazGo4rkhOPDhBu34T 
